---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import { site } from '../../data/site';
import { url } from '../../lib/url';

const bufferProject = `${site.repo}/tree/main/buffer`;
const bufferProduction = `${bufferProject}/jlcpcb/production_files`;
const bufferGerber = `${bufferProduction}/GERBER-buffer.zip`;
const bufferBom = `${bufferProduction}/BOM-buffer.csv`;
const bufferCpl = `${bufferProduction}/CPL-buffer.csv`;

const enclosure = [
  { label: 'Outer size', value: '180 × 150 × 55 mm' },
  { label: 'Wall thickness', value: '3 mm' },
  { label: 'Corner radius', value: '8 mm' },
  { label: 'Display', value: 'RPi Touch Display 2' },
  { label: 'Viewable screen', value: '125 × 74 mm' },
  { label: 'Footswitches', value: '4 × 12 mm, corner-inset' },
  { label: 'Encoder', value: 'rear-center rotary' },
  { label: 'Fixings', value: 'M3 heat-set inserts' },
];

const controlPins = [
  { fn: 'Footswitch 1 / slot 1', gpio: 'GPIO5', pin: '29' },
  { fn: 'Footswitch 2 / slot 2', gpio: 'GPIO6', pin: '31' },
  { fn: 'Footswitch 3 / slot 3', gpio: 'GPIO13', pin: '33' },
  { fn: 'Footswitch 4 / slot 4', gpio: 'GPIO16', pin: '36' },
  { fn: 'Encoder A', gpio: 'GPIO17', pin: '11' },
  { fn: 'Encoder B', gpio: 'GPIO22', pin: '15' },
  { fn: 'MIDI input (UART4 RX)', gpio: 'GPIO9', pin: '21' },
  { fn: 'Expression ADC alert', gpio: 'GPIO25', pin: '22' },
];
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Hardware"
  description="The parts, enclosure, and safe wiring plan for an Ardor Raspberry Pi pedal."
  lede="Ardor is built around a Raspberry Pi 4, a Codec Zero audio board, a touch display, and simple hands-free controls."
>
  <h2>Parts for a full pedal</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Raspberry Pi 4B.</li>
    <li>Raspberry Pi Codec Zero audio board.</li>
    <li>Raspberry Pi Touch Display 2.</li>
    <li>The Ardor minimal guitar input buffer board.</li>
    <li>Four momentary footswitches.</li>
    <li>One rotary encoder for master volume.</li>
    <li>A guitar input jack and a stereo audio output jack.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    You also need a microSD card and a suitable power supply. Download the{' '}
    <a href={url('/docs/getting-started')}>ready-made release image</a> before you build the pedal.
  </p>

  <div class="callout warn">
    <p>
      Disconnect power before you wire or change the pedal. Raspberry Pi control pins use
      3.3 V logic. Do not connect a higher voltage to a control pin. Connect the guitar to
      the input buffer before you connect it to the Codec Zero.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>What each part does</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Raspberry Pi 4B</td><td>Runs the pedal software and the browser manager.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Input buffer</td><td>Protects and buffers the guitar signal before the Codec Zero.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Codec Zero</td><td>Converts the buffered guitar signal to digital audio and sends audio to the output.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Touch Display 2</td><td>Shows presets, the signal path, controls, and the tuner.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Footswitches</td><td>Choose the four preset slots in the active bank.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Rotary encoder</td><td>Sets the master output volume.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Guitar input buffer</h2>
  <p>
    The{' '}<a href={bufferProject}>buffer project</a> is a separate, minimal KiCad board. It
    sits between the guitar jack and the Codec Zero. Connect the guitar jack to
    <code>GUITAR_IN</code>. Connect <code>GUITAR_OUT</code> to the Codec Zero input. Share
    ground, and follow the schematic for power.
  </p>
  <p>
    You can order the board with its SMD parts already fitted. Upload the
    <a href={bufferGerber}><code>GERBER-buffer.zip</code></a>,{' '}
    <a href={bufferBom}><code>BOM-buffer.csv</code></a>, and{' '}
    <a href={bufferCpl}><code>CPL-buffer.csv</code></a> files from the{' '}
    <a href={bufferProduction}>JLCPCB production folder</a> to the{' '}
    <a href="https://jlcpcb.com/">JLCPCB assembly service</a>. Review the parts and
    placement before you order.
  </p>
  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      The JLCPCB files cover the SMD parts. They do not include jacks, wires, or enclosure
      parts. Add the signal, power, and ground wires or connectors to the labeled board
      points after assembly.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Enclosure</h2>
  <p>
    The repository contains OpenSCAD files and 3MF meshes for a printable enclosure. The
    enclosure has space for the display, four footswitches, and the rear encoder.
  </p>
  <dl class="spec-grid">
    {enclosure.map((s) => (
      <div>
        <dt>{s.label}</dt>
        <dd class="mono">{s.value}</dd>
      </div>
    ))}
  </dl>

  <h2>Controls</h2>
  <p>
    Connect each footswitch and the encoder to the Raspberry Pi control header. The pedal
    uses the first four footswitches for preset slots. The encoder controls master volume.
    The display connects through the display cable. The Codec Zero connects through the
    Raspberry Pi header.
  </p>
  <p>
    Use the assembly files in the repository for the physical wiring. Test each switch and
    the encoder before you close the enclosure.
  </p>

  <h2>MIDI and expression control</h2>
  <p>
    An optional control circuit can add a TRS MIDI input and an expression pedal. These
    controls can choose preset slots, change banks, open the tuner, or control an effect
    parameter.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>MIDI message</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td class="mono">Program Change 0–3</td><td>Select preset slot 1–4 in the active bank.</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="mono">CC 0 / CC 32</td><td>Select bank 0–99.</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="mono">CC 20 ≥ 64</td><td>Open the tuner. A value below 64 closes it.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Software</h2>
  <p>
    The release image contains the pedal software, the browser manager, starter presets, and
    the audio setup. Follow <a href={url('/docs/getting-started')}>Getting Started</a> to
    write the image to a card. You do not need to build the software for a normal setup.
  </p>

  <details>
    <summary>Advanced: control pin map</summary>
    <p>
      Use this table when you make a control board. The physical pin numbers refer to the
      Raspberry Pi header.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>GPIO</th><th>Physical pin</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {controlPins.map((p) => (
          <tr><td>{p.fn}</td><td class="mono">{p.gpio}</td><td class="mono tnum">{p.pin}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Footswitches and the encoder connect to ground. The Raspberry Pi uses internal pull-up
      resistors. The pedal reads footswitches through <code>gpio-keys</code> and the encoder
      through <code>rotary-encoder</code>.
    </p>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Advanced: audio, display, and control buses</summary>
    <ul>
      <li>The Codec Zero uses I2S for audio.</li>
      <li>The Codec Zero uses I2C for control.</li>
      <li>The HAT EEPROM pins are reserved by the Codec Zero.</li>
      <li>The Touch Display 2 uses the DSI display connection.</li>
      <li>MIDI input uses UART4 RX on GPIO9.</li>
      <li>The expression pedal alert uses GPIO25.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Check the assembly files before you change the display or the audio board. A different
      board can use different pins.
    </p>
  </details>

  <style>
    .spec-grid {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 220px), 1fr));
      gap: 0;
      border: 1px solid var(--hairline);
      border-radius: var(--radius);
      overflow: hidden;
      margin: var(--sp-3) 0 var(--sp-4);
    }
    .spec-grid div {
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      gap: 0.2rem;
      padding: var(--sp-3);
      border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hairline);
      border-right: 1px solid var(--hairline);
    }
    .spec-grid dt {
      font-size: 0.66rem;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      letter-spacing: 0.1em;
      color: var(--muted-2);
    }
    .spec-grid dd {
      margin: 0;
      color: var(--text-strong);
    }
  </style>
</DocsLayout>
